N2  - Reliability engineering plays a critical role in ensuring safe operations in Advanced Air Mobility (AAM). This study presents a system-level optimization framework for evaluating and improving the reliability of a generic Command-and-Control (C2) system architecture based on the Mobile Testing and Evaluation Center (MTEC) unit developed by Parallax Advanced Research. The C2 architecture is decomposed into several primary subsystems, including power, communication, sensing, control (hardware and software), and data storage, allowing reliability to be evaluated at both the subsystem and overall system levels. The optimization model aims to maximize total system reliability, sensor detection probability, and detection range by modifying the sensing and control subsystems. The sensing subsystem includes six sensor types: radar, radio frequency (RF), Automatic Dependent Surveillance?Broadcast (ADS-B), acoustic sensors, Remote Identification (Remote ID), and optical cameras. The baseline control subsystem consists of eight primary workstations capable of supporting up to 20 UAVs. Additional workstations can be incorporated to improve overall system reliability. The decision variables include the number of sensors of each type and the number of additional workstations added to the control subsystem. The objective function maximizes system reliability, detection probability, and detection range while satisfying budget constraints and a minimum reliability requirement of 95%. The model is evaluated across budgets ranging from $30,000 to $150,000 and UAV fleet sizes from 20 to 50 vehicles. For a representative scenario with 20 UAVs and a $60,000 budget, the optimal configuration includes four backup workstations, sixteen ADS-B sensors, one Remote ID sensor, and one optical camera. This configuration achieves approximately 97% system reliability at a total cost of $59,793.80. The results demonstrate that both system budget and fleet size significantly influence reliability and optimal resource allocation.
